<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                     xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!-- ================================================================
         Borderless - an OVERLAY, merged on top of Themes/Dark.xaml, never loaded on its own.
         (Application.Resources resolves a key through its merged dictionaries last-one-wins, so every
         SurfaceX below beats the base theme's copy for anything that asks for it from outside that file.)

         It redefines only the structural surface keys: the chrome stops painting itself, so the background
         art - which MainWindow stretches behind the whole window in this mode - IS the app's background, and
         each region is told apart by a thin black outline instead of a filled grey card.

         Deliberately NOT touched: Bg0/Bg1/Bg2/Bg3, Card, CodeBg, Overlay, GlassBar. Buttons, popups, menus,
         your own messages, tool cards and code blocks keep their fills, because a transcript still has to be
         readable over somebody's wallpaper.

         Never merged in CLI mode - a flat black terminal has no art to reveal (AppSettings.IsBorderless).
         ================================================================ -->

    <!-- titlebar, sidebar, right panel / chat header + canvas / bridge pane box / pane header + composer bars -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceChrome" Color="Transparent" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceCanvas" Color="Transparent" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfacePane" Color="Transparent" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceBar" Color="Transparent" />

    <!-- The single chat's composer bubble, which is why a chat used to read as "not borderless" next to a Bridge
         on the same wallpaper. A pane's composer is a SurfaceBar strip, so it emptied out with the pane; the main
         one was a filled Bg2 slab and stayed one - and with every panel around it gone it became the biggest solid
         block on screen, sitting exactly where the eye lands. Empty it, and the two composers match. The bubble
         keeps its outline (and its accent focus ring), so it is still a box you type into; and it sits in the
         darkest band of the backdrop scrim, which is where the pane composer already reads fine. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceComposer" Color="Transparent" />

    <!-- the one thing that survives: a thin black line around and between every region. Without it the panes
         lose their edges entirely against a busy wallpaper and the layout stops reading as panes at all.
         Slightly translucent so it sits ON the art rather than punching a hole in it. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceOutline" Color="#D9000000" />

    <!-- Chips that float in a bar (the account usage pills, the parked-pane restore chip) lose their FILL and keep
         a hairline instead - the opposite of what this used to do. Keeping the fill was meant to back their text,
         but with the bar behind them no longer painted it left the Claude/Codex/Kimi/Grok readouts as the only
         filled slabs in a borderless bridge header, which is exactly what stops it reading as borderless. Emptied,
         they match the bare glyph buttons beside them; the outline is what keeps a chip reading as a chip rather
         than as loose text, and it is the same black hairline every other region gets above. Hover and the open
         state still fill (their templates name Bg3 directly), so the pills answer the pointer either way. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ChipFill" Color="Transparent" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ChipOutline" Color="#D9000000" />

    <!-- square the panes off: a rounded card with no fill reads as a mistake, a rectangle reads as a frame -->
    <CornerRadius x:Key="RadPane">0</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="RadPaneTop">0</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="RadPaneBottom">0</CornerRadius>

    <!-- Send button: the circle as an OUTLINE, not a filled disc. With every panel gone the solid blue disc
         was the last filled slab on screen, but losing the circle altogether left the arrow floating with no
         target to aim at - so the ring stays and only the fill goes. Accent ring, accent arrow, and a soft
         wash on hover so it still answers the pointer. Interrupt is unaffected: while a turn is running that
         state overrides all of these with its own red outline, which is the same shape in a different colour. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SendButtonFill" Color="Transparent" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SendButtonFillHover" Color="#244C8DF5" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SendButtonGlyph" Color="#4C8DF5" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SendButtonRing" Color="#4C8DF5" />

</ResourceDictionary>
